        <body>
            <div xml:id="d143-a1">

                <pb facs="0343" n="323" sameAs="frus:frus1864p2/pg323"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Confidential.]</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Savage to Mr.
                        Seward.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 207.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">United States Consulate General,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Havana,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-09-22"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September 22,</hi> 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> The Spanish brig Yucatan, Rey, master, was
                    cleared yesterday ostensibly for Fernando Po and Annobon (Spanish possessions in
                    Africa) by a Portuguese, named Miranda. The cargo <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >cleared</hi> at the custom-house consists of 365 packages of provisions, 10
                    boxes of sugar, 315 arrobes of coffee; 20 M cigars, 1 case of Cologne-water, 1
                    case, (medicine-chest)) 20 empty casks, and 340 pipes of rum— aguardiente; this
                    may be water.</p>
                <pb facs="0344" n="324" xml:id="pg324"/>
                <p>I am informed that the real person interested is his excellency the post captain,
                    Du Francisco Marty y Torrens, and that the object of the voyage is to bring
                    African negroes. Marty has not been by any means a stranger to this kind of
                    business in the course of his long life.</p>
                <p>I am also advised that a steamer with a load of negroes is now momently expected
                    from Africa, under command of the notorious slaver captain, Eugenio Vinas. The
                    parties charged with the duty of receiving the expedition have already gone to
                    the place where it is to be landed. It is said that Don Jose. Oarreras is the
                    party interested, which is quite probable. If this is the case, we may say that
                    the principal owners are Don Salvador Sarnd, Brigadier Mu-noz, &amp;c.</p>
                <p>Should the expedition be landed I will endeavor to ascertain all particulars
                    connected therewith and send them to the department.</p>
                <p>With great respect, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>THOS. SAVAGE.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State; Washington.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
